<v Speaker 2>You cannot merely study the medicine and expect to be cured.

27
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:43.070
<v Speaker 1>I see.

28
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.670
<v Speaker 1>So we begin with a specific detailed report from the text.

29
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.350
<v Speaker 1>The master introduces the master's disciple of Khurasan.

30
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:51.710
<v Speaker 1>May Allah be pleased with him.

31
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:52.270
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

32
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:55.470
<v Speaker 1>And this disciple has returned to the house of the Khurasanian teacher.

33
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.630
<v Speaker 1>May Allah shower his mercy upon him.

34
00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:58.910
<v Speaker 2>That is the setting, yeah.

35
00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.430
<v Speaker 1>The disciple has been away for some time and he brings back something unusual.

36
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.150
<v Speaker 1>He has not gathered theories from reading volumes of theology while he was away.

37
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:07.910
<v Speaker 2>No, he hasn't.

38
00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:12.390
<v Speaker 1>Instead, he has spent his time in a long, unbroken observation of

39
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:20.110
<v Speaker 1>He has been watching how they live, what they chase with such desperation, and what ultimately eats their hearts.

40
00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.430
<v Speaker 2>And he returns with a rigid methodology.

41
00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:29.230
<v Speaker 2>He reports 8 benefits to his teacher, and every single one follows the exact same three-part structure.

42
00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.190
<v Speaker 1>It is a brilliant formula, a spiritual diagnosis, really.

43
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.310
<v Speaker 1>It absolutely is.

44
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:37.710
<v Speaker 2>He says, basically I looked at the people and saw a specific condition.

45
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.630
<v Speaker 2>Then I contemplated a specific verse from the Quran.

46
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.430
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, I performed a specific action.

47
00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.550
<v Speaker 1>It's so mechanical.

48
00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:46.510
<v Speaker 2>Very mechanical.

49
00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.150
<v Speaker 2>The Quran acts as a corrective lens here.

50
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.870
<v Speaker 2>It makes the invisible diseases of the people of the world clearly visible to him.

51
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:57.150
<v Speaker 1>And that therefore, Claus, demands an immediate, tangible change in behavior.

52
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.190
<v Speaker 2>Yes, exactly.

53
00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.310
<v Speaker 2>He looks at the world, he weighs the observation against the book, and he alters his life immediately.

54
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.230
<v Speaker 1>So let us walk through these eight benefits.

55
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:06.990
<v Speaker 1>The first observation is Stark.

56
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.070
<v Speaker 1>He looks at the people of the world and sees that everyone has a beloved.

57
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:16.190
<v Speaker 1>Every single person has something or someone they cherish, something they direct their entire life toward.

58
00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.110
<v Speaker 1>But he notices that these beloveds are terribly frail.

59
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.950
<v Speaker 2>Frail is the exact word.

60
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.230
<v Speaker 1>Some accompany the lover only until a fatal illness strikes.

61
00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.670
<v Speaker 1>Some are faithful enough to accompany them until death itself.

62
00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.630
<v Speaker 1>Others might make it to the edge, the burial site.

63
00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.030
<v Speaker 2>But that is the absolute limit.

64
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.190
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, because after burial, all of those loved ones return home.

65
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:45.550
<v Speaker 1>None of them descend into the grave to offer companionship in the darkness, the solitude, the narrowness, and the terror of that place.

66
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.950
<v Speaker 1>The lover is left entirely alone.

67
00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.510
<v Speaker 2>The disciple looks at the terminal point of human companionship.

68
00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.790
<v Speaker 2>He sees that the very nature of worldly love is bounded by the edge of the grave.

69
00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.310
<v Speaker 1>The edge of the dirt itself.

70
00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:57.830
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

71
00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.310
<v Speaker 2>The dirt is the boundary.

72
00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:10.910
<v Speaker 2>The disciple realizes that the only beloved possessing the qualities required to actually descend into the earth to illuminate its darkness and to remain a partner through the day of judgment is his own righteous deeds.

73
00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:12.350
<v Speaker 1>So his action is absolute.

74
00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:12.830
<v Speaker 2>It is.

75
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.670
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, he makes his righteous deeds his one true beloved.

76
00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.030
<v Speaker 1>I have to challenge this here.

77
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.910
<v Speaker 1>Human connection is fundamental to our nature.

78
00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:21.710
<v Speaker 2>Of course it is.

79
00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:29.630
<v Speaker 1>If you are listening to this and looking at your own family, the idea of turning your back on human love feels incredibly cold.

80
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.070
<v Speaker 1>Is the disciple saying that loving people is a failure?

81
00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.270
<v Speaker 2>No, it is not a failure of love.

82
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.190
<v Speaker 2>It is a failure of durability.

83
00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:38.750
<v Speaker 1>Durability.

84
00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:39.310
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

85
00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.390
<v Speaker 2>The disciple is not advocating for a cold heart.

86
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:50.590
<v Speaker 2>He is addressing the human tendency to build our ultimate sense of security on foundations that simply cannot cross the final threshold with us.

87
00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:51.710
<v Speaker 1>Because they have to walk back home.

88
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:52.510
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

89
00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.350
<v Speaker 2>They have to walk away from the burial site.

90
00:04:54.990 --> 00:05:01.830
<v Speaker 2>If you invest the entirety of your soul's capital into mortals, you will bankrupt yourself at the grave because mortals belong to the earth above.

91
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:03.310
<v Speaker 1>And righteous deeds do not.

92
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:10.350
<v Speaker 2>Righteous deeds, acts of charity, pure prayer, selfless service, these are woven from a fabric that survives the soil.

93
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:18.110
<v Speaker 2>To make a deed your beloved means to treat that action with the devotion, the care, and the prioritization that you normally reserve for a person.

94
00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:22.390
<v Speaker 2>And when the Chrysanian teacher hears this, he immediately confirms it.

95
00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:24.870
<v Speaker 2>He says, bravo, you have said a most excellent thing.

96
00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:32.190
<v Speaker 1>So if righteous deeds are the only companion that can survive the graves, what stops us from accumulating them right now?

97
00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.550
<v Speaker 2>Well, the text points immediately to the internal enemy.

98
00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.750
<v Speaker 1>The second benefit?

99
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:37.230
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

100
00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.550
<v Speaker 2>The second benefit focuses on the inner life.

101
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.350
<v Speaker 2>The disciple looks at the people and sees everyone running relentlessly after their pleasures.

102
00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.310
<v Speaker 1>He sees them completely subservient to their desires.

103
00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:49.390
<v Speaker 1>Practically enslaved by them.

104
00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:52.030
<v Speaker 2>Laboring day and night just to feed their appetite.

105
00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.630
<v Speaker 1>This is the nafs, yes.

106
00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:54.270
<v Speaker 2>The nafs.

107
00:05:54.510 --> 00:06:01.870
<v Speaker 2>It is the lower self, the primal appetite within the human being that actively incites a person toward evil and instant gratification.

108
00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.310
<v Speaker 1>It is a constant hunger.

109
00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.150
<v Speaker 2>A hunger that cannot be satisfied.

110
00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:12.110
<v Speaker 2>The disciple weighs this universal subservience against a specific verse, Quran 79.4041.

111
00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:17.350
<v Speaker 2>This verse speaks of the one who stands in awe of his sustainer's presence and restraints

112
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.030
<v Speaker 2>drains his inner self from base and evil desires.

113
00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.870
<v Speaker 1>But restraining the inner self sounds like a constant exhausting battle.

114
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:24.870
<v Speaker 2>It is exhausting.

115
00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.750
<v Speaker 1>How does the disciple actually do this?

116
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:31.710
<v Speaker 1>I mean, we all know we should eat less, consume less, want less, but the biological Dr.

117
00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:32.430
<v Speaker 1>is overwhelming.

118
00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:36.030
<v Speaker 2>You have to look at the exact mechanism of his therefore action.

119
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.870
<v Speaker 2>The disciple's response is not to negotiate with the lower self.

120
00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.390
<v Speaker 1>Because negotiation fails.

121
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:42.030
<v Speaker 2>Always.

122
00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:47.070
<v Speaker 2>If you negotiate with the appetite, the appetite always wins because it never tires of wanting.

123
00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.070
<v Speaker 2>Instead, he takes a firm stand.

124
00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.030
<v Speaker 1>A total refusal.

125
00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:50.510
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

126
00:06:50.830 --> 00:07:02.190
<v Speaker 2>He resolves to oppose the lower self, refusing to fulfill any of its desires until it is disciplined to the point where it begins to find its peace and satisfaction entirely in the worship of Allah.

127
00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.430
<v Speaker 1>So it is a total withdrawal of provisions.

128
00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:06.030
<v Speaker 2>Think of it like breaking a wild horse.

129
00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:06.630
<v Speaker 2>You do not

130
00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:07.390
<v Speaker 2>reason with it.

131
00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.870
<v Speaker 2>You withhold the rain until it accepts the saddle.

132
00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:13.470
<v Speaker 2>By starving the base desires, the appetite shrinks.

133
00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.070
<v Speaker 1>And a new, refined taste for worship begins to emerge.

134
00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:17.870
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

135
00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:22.430
<v Speaker 1>This internal starvation of the lower self connects directly to his third observation.

136
00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.790
<v Speaker 1>This one moves from the internal appetite to the external hoarding of provisions.

137
00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:28.990
<v Speaker 2>The material wealth.

138
00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:29.710
<v Speaker 1>Yeah.

139
00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.910
<v Speaker 1>He watches the painful labor people endure.

140
00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:38.910
<v Speaker 1>He sees them working themselves to physical exhaustion to amass the possessions of this mortal world.

141
00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.630
<v Speaker 1>Money, goods, physical wealth.

142
00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:45.630
<v Speaker 2>And worse, he observes how greatly pleased they are to have amassed it.

143
00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:49.950
<v Speaker 1>Hoarding it as if it will protect them from the very grave we just discussed in the first benefit.

144
00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:53.950
<v Speaker 2>What is crucial here is the verse he applies to this painful labor.

145
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.670
<v Speaker 2>It is Quran 16.96.

146
00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:02.270
<v Speaker 2>All that is with you is bound to come to an end, whereas all that which is with Allah is everlasting.

147
00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.670
<v Speaker 1>The psychological mechanism here is profound.

148
00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:05.390
<v Speaker 2>It really is.

149
00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.270
<v Speaker 2>Humans hoard wealth because they fear scarcity.

150
00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.430
<v Speaker 2>But the disciple looks at the wealth he has amassed and applies the logic of the verse.

151
00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:15.230
<v Speaker 1>Anything kept in mortal hands is guaranteed to decay.

152
00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.430
<v Speaker 2>Decay, be stolen, or just be left behind.

153
00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.790
<v Speaker 1>And his action is incredibly drastic here.

154
00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:27.470
<v Speaker 1>He takes whatever material possessions he has accumulated and distributes them entirely in the way of Allah to the pious indigence and the needy.

155
00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:28.910
<v Speaker 2>Yes, he empties his hands.

156
00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:32.150
<v Speaker 1>But wait, let us be entirely practical for a moment.

157
00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:33.710
<v Speaker 1>That sounds completely reckless.

158
00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.990
<v Speaker 1>How is a person supposed to survive or provide for dependents if they distribute everything?

159
00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.670
<v Speaker 1>Is this text demanding literal, absolute destitution?

160
00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.150
<v Speaker 2>No, it is demanding a complete psychological break from the illusion of ownership.

161
00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.950
<v Speaker 2>The text uses very precise language here.

162
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:57.350
<v Speaker 2>By distributing this wealth to the needy, those possessions cease to be mortal wealth.

163
00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:58.190
<v Speaker 1>What do they become?

164
00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.750
<v Speaker 2>They become a permanent deposit with Allah.

165
00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:04.150
<v Speaker 2>They become a source of immense good and a ransom for salvation.

166
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.990
<v Speaker 1>So he is not throwing his wealth away.

167
00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:07.950
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

168
00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.110
<v Speaker 2>He is transferring his assets.

169
00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.550
<v Speaker 2>He is sending his wealth ahead to a place where it cannot be lost.

170
00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.350
<v Speaker 2>To the eyes of the world, he is emptying his hands.

171
00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:21.070
<v Speaker 2>But to the eyes of the disciple, he is securing his fortune in an impregnable vault.

172
00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:22.430
<v Speaker 1>If you hold on to it, you lose it.

173
00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:23.070
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

174
00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.470
<v Speaker 2>If you give it to the sustainer, you keep it forever.

175
00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.070
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to the 4th benefit.

176
00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.470
<v Speaker 1>Because once that mortal wealth is accumulated, it breeds a dangerous fiction.

177
00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:32.910
<v Speaker 2>The fiction of nobility.

178
00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:33.710
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

179
00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:38.030
<v Speaker 1>The disciple catalogs the fictions of true nobility that people construct.

180
00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:44.590
<v Speaker 1>He sees people believing that grandeur and honor come from belonging to a large, powerful nation or tribe.

181
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.390
<v Speaker 2>Or they believe it comes from an abundance of wealth and family.

182
00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:52.270
<v Speaker 1>Or, more destructively, they believe it lies in displaying anger, in beating,

183
00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.830
<v Speaker 1>killing and pillage to prove dominance.

184
00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:59.470
<v Speaker 2>Some even believe nobility lies in wasteful spending just to show they can.

185
00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:04.670
<v Speaker 1>They take immense pride in these fictions, boasting to one another in the town squares and the marketplaces.

186
00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.590
<v Speaker 2>These are the false conjectures of the world.

187
00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:11.550
<v Speaker 2>People exhaust their entire lives of holding these illusions, terrified of losing face.

188
00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.270
<v Speaker 1>They are terrified of being seen as less than their neighbors.

189
00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.550
<v Speaker 2>And against all of those fictions, the disciple brings Quran 49.13.

190
00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:25.470
<v Speaker 2>This verse definitively declares that the noblest in the sight of Allah is the one who is most deeply conscious and fearful of Him.

191
00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:32.830
<v Speaker 1>The phonetic pronunciation for this concept is takiwa, pious reverence, consciousness, awe and fear of Allah.

192
00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:33.630
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

193
00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.110
<v Speaker 2>The disciple realizes that the entire hierarchical structure of the

194
00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.270
<v Speaker 2>world is built on a lie.

195
00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:44.430
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, his action is to adopt tequwa as the single correct measure of nobility.

196
00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.950
<v Speaker 2>He completely discards the false conjectures of the world.

197
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:51.710
<v Speaker 2>Think about the immense psychological relief in this action.

198
00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.390
<v Speaker 1>The relief of not having to perform.

199
00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:54.190
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

200
00:10:54.350 --> 00:11:04.990
<v Speaker 2>If you no longer measure your worth by your tribal affiliation, your bank account, or your ability to intimidate others, the crushing burden of maintaining that worldly image just vanishes.

201
00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:06.510
<v Speaker 2>You secure the highest

202
00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.510
<v Speaker 2>in the sight of a law by abandoning the theater of human pride.

203
00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:14.830
<v Speaker 1>The theater of human pride is exactly what causes the friction we see in the 5th benefit.

204
00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.270
<v Speaker 2>The complaining against one another.

205
00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:16.750
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

206
00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:19.310
<v Speaker 1>The disciple observes people constantly complaining.

207
00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.030
<v Speaker 1>He identifies the root causes of all this noise.

208
00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:29.310
<v Speaker 1>envy, jealousy, and enmity born from competition over greatness, grandeur, possessions, wealth, and even knowledge.

209
00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:32.270
<v Speaker 2>Everyone is looking at what their neighbor has, trying to outdo the other.

210
00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.550
<v Speaker 1>And it eats them alive.

211
00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:38.430
<v Speaker 2>To cure this universal disease of envy, he contemplates Quran 43.32.

212
00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:44.270
<v Speaker 2>This verse states, it is we who distribute their means of livelihood among them in the life of this world.

213
00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:47.070
<v Speaker 1>I want to pause on the mechanism of this cure.

214
00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.910
<v Speaker 1>Because telling a human being to simply stop competing,

215
00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:52.430
<v Speaker 1>feels impossible.

216
00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:58.430
<v Speaker 1>It is hardwired into our biology to look at the resources of others and feel a sense of lack.

217
00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.830
<v Speaker 2>It is a deep survival instinct, yes.

218
00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:06.350
<v Speaker 1>So how does reading that verse actually cure the burning feeling of envy in the chest?

219
00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.790
<v Speaker 2>It cures it by destroying the illusion of control.

220
00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:10.670
<v Speaker 1>What do you mean by that?

221
00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:16.830
<v Speaker 2>The verse establishes that all possessions, ranks, and livelihoods were appointed from the very beginning of creation.

222
00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:18.670
<v Speaker 2>The distribution is already finished.

223
00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:19.470
<v Speaker 1>It is settled.

224
00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:20.190
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

225
00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:32.830
<v Speaker 2>If you are deeply envious of your neighbor's sprawling fields or heavy purse, you are operating under the false belief that those fields or that purse could have been yours if only you had worked harder or if they had not taken it.

226
00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:37.470
<v Speaker 1>But the disciple realizes that no mortal has any control over this distribution.

227
00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.750
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, his action is to completely cease competing.

228
00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:45.630
<v Speaker 1>The anxiety of feeling you missed out is cured by knowing it was never written for you in the 1st place.

229
00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:46.430
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

230
00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:50.030
<v Speaker 2>He surrenders every jealousy and harbor of enmity.

231
00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:54.910
<v Speaker 2>He remains perfectly content with Allah's distribution and appointed destiny.

232
00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.070
<v Speaker 1>And he lives in peace with all of creation.

233
00:12:57.070 --> 00:13:05.630
<v Speaker 2>The struggle for dominance is abandoned, not because he is defeated, but because he recognizes that fighting against the pre-existing decree is absolute madness.

234
00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.550
<v Speaker 1>But even if you surrender your envy,

235
00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.270
<v Speaker 1>Other people might still hold enmity toward you.

236
00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:12.830
<v Speaker 2>That leads right to the 6th benefit.

237
00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:13.310
<v Speaker 1>Right.

238
00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:18.110
<v Speaker 1>In the 6th benefit, he sees that every person holds enmity towards someone else for some reason or another.

239
00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.750
<v Speaker 1>People are fighting the wrong battles, constantly turning their weapons on their fellow creatures.

240
00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.190
<v Speaker 2>They are completely distracted.

241
00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:30.350
<v Speaker 2>The disciple turns to Quran 35.6, which identifies the true avowed enemy.

242
00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:33.470
<v Speaker 1>The phonetic pronunciation for this adversary is Shaytanan.

243
00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:34.030
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

244
00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:40.830
<v Speaker 2>The disciple's action is to regard this single invisible enemy as his only adversary, refusing all of his promptings.

245
00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:44.750
<v Speaker 1>Why is it so much easier for people to fight each other than to fight this actual enemy?

246
00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.590
<v Speaker 2>Because human enemies are visible.

247
00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:52.510
<v Speaker 2>You can yell at a neighbor, you can strike a rival, you can visibly win a dispute over land.

248
00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:53.590
<v Speaker 1>It is tangible.

249
00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:54.270
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

250
00:13:54.590 --> 00:14:01.870
<v Speaker 2>But the true enemy operates entirely in the unseen, whispering into the chest, inciting the very anger and pride we just discussed.

251
00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:09.710
<v Speaker 1>So the disciple stops holding enmity against his fellow humans because he realizes they are not the source of the conflict.

252
00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:11.790
<v Speaker 2>They are just victims of the same whispers.

253
00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:16.990
<v Speaker 2>Instead, he devotes himself entirely to cultivating obedience to Allah on the straight path.

254
00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.670
<v Speaker 2>He aligns himself with the ancient covenant made with the children of Adam.

255
00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:24.390
<v Speaker 1>To never obey the avowed enemy and to worship Allah alone.

256
00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:29.110
<v Speaker 1>This shift in focus brings up a highly practical dilemma in the 7th benefit.

257
00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.430
<v Speaker 2>The issue of survival.

258
00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:30.910
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

259
00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:35.630
<v Speaker 1>It requires immense focus to fight the actual enemy and maintain worship.

260
00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:42.430
<v Speaker 1>How does a disciple focus entirely on obedience when the pressures of survival and livelihood are constantly bearing down?

261
00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:43.870
<v Speaker 2>It is a crushing pressure.

262
00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:47.070
<v Speaker 1>The disciple sees people degrading themselves in the race for earnings.

263
00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:50.430
<v Speaker 1>They are consumed by intense effort in the pursuit of sustenance.

264
00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:55.550
<v Speaker 2>In their panic to feed themselves and their families, they blur the lines between the permissible and the forbidden.

265
00:14:55.710 --> 00:15:01.870
<v Speaker 1>They pursue doubtful earnings, lying, cheating, and humiliating themselves just to survive.

266
00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:05.390
<v Speaker 2>The panic of survival is the greatest thief of spiritual focus.

267
00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:07.430
<v Speaker 2>Disciple anchors' response in.

268
00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:10.650
<v Speaker 2>in the absolute promise of Quran 11.6.

269
00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:16.250
<v Speaker 2>And there is no living creature on earth whose sustenance is not the responsibility of Allah.

270
00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.570
<v Speaker 1>Again, I have to challenge this.

271
00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:18.970
<v Speaker 2>Go ahead.

272
00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:24.890
<v Speaker 1>If you were sitting in a house with no food, reading that sustenance is guaranteed does not put bread on the table.

273
00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.810
<v Speaker 1>If you stop striving, you starve.

274
00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:31.370
<v Speaker 1>How does the disciple reconcile the necessity of physical labor with this verse?

275
00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:35.050
<v Speaker 2>It is vital to understand that the text does not forbid physical effort.

276
00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:38.730
<v Speaker 2>It forbids the internal degradation and panic associated with it.

277
00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:39.770
<v Speaker 1>The internal panic.

278
00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:40.170
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

279
00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:44.010
<v Speaker 2>The disciple recognizes that he is one of those living creatures present upon the earth.

280
00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.530
<v Speaker 2>His action is profound.

281
00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:50.730
<v Speaker 2>He devotes himself to the worship of Allah and holds firm to the certitude that his sustenance will be brought to him.

282
00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:55.610
<v Speaker 1>Because the responsibility of provision belongs to the sustainer, not the servant.

283
00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:56.450
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

284
00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:59.450
<v Speaker 2>You go out into the field, you plant the seed, you swing the hammer.

285
00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:00.810
<v Speaker 2>That is your physical duty.

286
00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:05.770
<v Speaker 2>But your heart must remain utterly detached from the anxiety of the outcome.

287
00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:14.170
<v Speaker 1>So degrading oneself or lying or pursuing forbidden wealth in pursuit of what is already guaranteed is a failure of certitude.

288
00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:16.650
<v Speaker 2>It is a fundamental failure of certitude.

289
00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:19.610
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us directly to the final eighth benefit.

290
00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:21.850
<v Speaker 1>Where does ultimate reliance lie?

291
00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:23.450
<v Speaker 2>The fragile supports.

292
00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:23.850
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

293
00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:29.290
<v Speaker 1>He observes the people and sees that everyone's reliance rests on something incredibly fragile.

294
00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:30.690
<v Speaker 1>Some place their trust

295
00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:34.490
<v Speaker 1>entirely in their material possessions, hoarding gold clones and goods.

296
00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:40.810
<v Speaker 2>Others rely upon mortal people, placing their hope in wealthy patrons, powerful kings, or family members.

297
00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:42.930
<v Speaker 1>Hoping those mortals will save them or elevate them.

298
00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:46.810
<v Speaker 2>He concludes this long looking at the world with Quran 65.3.

299
00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:51.610
<v Speaker 2>For everyone who places his or her trust in Allah, he alone is sufficient.

300
00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:56.970
<v Speaker 1>The disciple lists the fragile supports of the world, wealth that can be stolen, patrons who will eventually die.

301
00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.970
<v Speaker 2>And he adopts complete reliance upon the one who suffices in every matter.

302
00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:08.330
<v Speaker 1>And when the Qurasanian teacher hears these eight profound shifts in behavior, he delivers a definitive verdict.

303
00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:08.970
<v Speaker 2>He does.

304
00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:18.250
<v Speaker 1>He states that he has looked into the Torah revealed to Moses, the Gospel revealed to Prophet Jesus, the Psalms revealed to Prophet David, and the Holy Quran.

305
00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:19.570
<v Speaker 2>All 4 books of God.

306
00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:23.530
<v Speaker 1>He testifies that all four of these books speak of these very 8 acts.

307
00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:26.730
<v Speaker 2>The master explains that the balance holds perfectly.

308
00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.370
<v Speaker 2>On one side of the scale, you have four books of God.

309
00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:32.250
<v Speaker 2>On the other side, you have these eight performed acts.

310
00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:35.770
<v Speaker 1>It is not a sprawling, impossible curriculum to memorize.

311
00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:39.530
<v Speaker 2>No, it is a precise remedy applied to the observed diseases of the heart.

312
00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:46.730
<v Speaker 2>The teacher is saying that whoever acts upon these eight principles is as one who has acted upon the core truth of all four holy books.

313
00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:48.490
<v Speaker 1>We have walked through the diagnosis.

314
00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:50.250
<v Speaker 1>Now the spine sentence returns.

315
00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:53.530
<v Speaker 2>Take what is written here and steadily put it into practice.

316
00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:58.570
<v Speaker 2>And through that practice, those matters which you do not yet understand will become clear to you.

317
00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.370
<v Speaker 1>We return to the absolute necessity of practice.

318
00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:05.290
<v Speaker 1>But if you look closely at these eight demands, they are terrifying.

319
00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.130
<v Speaker 2>They are incredibly difficult.

320
00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:12.570
<v Speaker 1>Give away your wealth, starve your lower self, stop competing with the world, stop relying on mortals.

321
00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.850
<v Speaker 1>How does a disciple actually maintain this practice?

322
00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:19.530
<v Speaker 1>The gravity of the world is immense and human beings are weak.

323
00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:24.090
<v Speaker 2>They are, and the master does not leave the seeker to walk this severe path alone.

324
00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.730
<v Speaker 2>Which brings us to the second braided strand of this text.

325
00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:28.570
<v Speaker 1>This condition.

326
00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.130
<v Speaker 1>The presence of the perfected guide.

327
00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:35.530
<v Speaker 2>The text uses a very precise analogy here, the analogy of the farmer.

328
00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:36.730
<v Speaker 1>Let's just look at that analogy.

329
00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:45.050
<v Speaker 2>When a farmer looks at a field and sees unnecessary weeds springing up among the grain, he does not just observe them, and he certainly does not read a book about them.

330
00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:46.810
<v Speaker 1>He pulls them out by the root and throws them away.

331
00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:47.530
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

332
00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.130
<v Speaker 2>Then he waters the crop and nourishes it so that it grows well.

333
00:18:51.690 --> 00:19:00.330
<v Speaker 2>In the exact same way, at every stage of the journey, the traveler on the path of Allah has no remedy except through a perfected spiritual guide.

334
00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:04.010
<v Speaker 1>I want to dwell on the physical reality of that farmer analogy.

335
00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:05.290
<v Speaker 2>The violence of it.

336
00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:06.490
<v Speaker 1>Yes, exactly.

337
00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.130
<v Speaker 1>Pulling a weed by the root is a violent action.

338
00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:10.490
<v Speaker 1>It disrupts the soil.

339
00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:11.370
<v Speaker 1>It leaves a hole.

340
00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.050
<v Speaker 1>If the seeker is the field and the guide is the farmer,

341
00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:18.090
<v Speaker 1>This means the process of being guided is going to hurt.

342
00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.290
<v Speaker 2>It is going to hurt deeply.

343
00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:26.250
<v Speaker 1>The guide is going to look at your life, find the things you are deeply attached to your pride, your anger, your subtle jealousies, and rip them out of you.

344
00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:30.490
<v Speaker 2>It is not a gentle process of offering comforting affirmations.

345
00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:36.010
<v Speaker 2>The guide pulls the evil qualities from within the seeker and replaces them with noble ones.

346
00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:43.370
<v Speaker 2>Because this process requires submitting your very soul to the hands of another, the stakes are incredibly high.

347
00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.090
<v Speaker 1>But where does the authority of this guide come from?

348
00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:50.130
<v Speaker 1>A person cannot just declare themselves a farmer of souls.

349
00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:50.890
<v Speaker 2>No, they cannot.

350
00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:56.490
<v Speaker 1>If someone walks into your field and starts tearing up the earth, you need to know they possess the right to do so.

351
00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:59.450
<v Speaker 1>The text emphasizes an unbroken chain.

352
00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:06.250
<v Speaker 2>It explains the binding pledge stretching all the way back to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

353
00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.290
<v Speaker 1>So the seeker is not being asked to invent a new path.

354
00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:12.410
<v Speaker 2>Or assemble one from fragments of different philosophies.

355
00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:17.610
<v Speaker 2>The seeker must find a guide in whom the original living path still breathes.

356
00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:21.370
<v Speaker 2>The authority is transmitted through direct, unbroken lineage.

357
00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:29.370
<v Speaker 1>And because there are always imposters who claim this authority, the text outlines a severe catalog of qualifications to spot a deceiver.

358
00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.290
<v Speaker 2>The master warns sharply here.

359
00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:35.610
<v Speaker 2>He says that every guide is a scholar, but not every scholar is a perfected guide.

360
00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.530
<v Speaker 1>Simply knowing the text does not qualify you to pull the weeds.

361
00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:39.210
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

362
00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.770
<v Speaker 2>No foolish or misguided person can falsely claim this role

363
00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:45.850
<v Speaker 2>for material gain if the seeker knows what to look for.

364
00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:48.010
<v Speaker 1>So what are these specifications?

365
00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:49.610
<v Speaker 2>They are intensely strict.

366
00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.610
<v Speaker 2>You can weigh any claimant against this catalog.

367
00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:57.450
<v Speaker 2>First, the guide must have turned completely away from the love of the world, the love of honor, and worldly rank.

368
00:20:57.450 --> 00:20:58.730
<v Speaker 1>He cannot be seeking fame.

369
00:20:58.730 --> 00:20:59.370
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

370
00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:02.250
<v Speaker 2>He must eat little, sleep little, and pray abundantly.

371
00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:04.090
<v Speaker 2>Then look at his inward character.

372
00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:12.770
<v Speaker 2>He must possess a light from the messenger of Allah that has utterly purged him of miserliness, jealousy, worldly expectation, anger.

373
00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:18.250
<v Speaker 1>He must be a person who has already undergone the brutal weeding process himself.

374
00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:21.290
<v Speaker 2>If these marks are present, he is a true deputy.

375
00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:26.170
<v Speaker 2>If they are absent, regardless of how beautifully he speaks, the title is a lie.

376
00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:32.570
<v Speaker 1>Even if a seeker finds a guide who meets all these inward and outward qualifications, there is a boundary.

377
00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:39.450
<v Speaker 1>The text draws an absolute bright line regarding what the guide can and cannot ask of the disciple.

378
00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.330
<v Speaker 2>Yes, the bright line is the phonetic boundary of the Shah Ariyah.

379
00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:44.570
<v Speaker 1>The sacred law.

380
00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:49.690
<v Speaker 2>A true guide never, under any circumstances, commands what the law of Allah forbids.

381
00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:51.930
<v Speaker 2>The law is the ultimate perimeter.

382
00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:56.890
<v Speaker 1>If the guide commands a disciple to do something forbidden by the law, the boundary is crossed.

383
00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:00.170
<v Speaker 2>And the guide is instantaneously proven to be a false imposter.

384
00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.290
<v Speaker 2>The law holds the absolute perimeter of the relationship.

385
00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:08.970
<v Speaker 2>It is the diagnostic test that separates the master from the deceiver, protecting the disciple from abuse.

386
00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:14.730
<v Speaker 1>If the guide is true and commands strictly within the law, the disciple must observe a demanding etiquette.

387
00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.290
<v Speaker 2>The outward and inward etiquette.

388
00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:17.770
<v Speaker 1>Let us start with the outward.

389
00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.530
<v Speaker 1>Outwardly, this means surrendering your own opinions.

390
00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:29.770
<v Speaker 1>Even if an argument arises and the disciple thinks the guide has overlooked something, he does not raise an objection or dispute the matter.

391
00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:30.690
<v Speaker 2>He stays silent.

392
00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.130
<v Speaker 1>It also dictates physical respect.

393
00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:39.690
<v Speaker 1>The disciple does not linger stationed at the place of prayer at all times just to show off devotion.

394
00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:44.010
<v Speaker 1>When the prayer is done, the disciple rolls up the prayer mat and leaves.

395
00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:49.850
<v Speaker 2>The disciple does not perform lengthy extra prayers in front of the guide just to be seen as pious.

396
00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:53.610
<v Speaker 1>The outward etiquette is about removing the performance of the ego.

397
00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:59.130
<v Speaker 1>But inwardly, the etiquette is even more exacting and much more difficult to maintain.

398
00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:00.410
<v Speaker 2>It is the hardest part.

399
00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:06.170
<v Speaker 1>Whatever the disciple hears or sees outwardly from the guide, there cannot be the slightest doubt entertained in the heart.

400
00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:08.050
<v Speaker 2>This is where it becomes incredibly difficult.

401
00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:10.090
<v Speaker 2>Human beings naturally doubt.

402
00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:11.450
<v Speaker 1>We second guess everything.

403
00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:18.650
<v Speaker 2>The text says that if the disciple doubts the guide inwardly while remaining physically present and outwardly compliant, it is hypocrisy.

404
00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:20.650
<v Speaker 1>Inward and outward most agree perfectly.

405
00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:24.170
<v Speaker 2>The remedy for this internal discord is honest withdrawal.

406
00:23:24.490 --> 00:23:26.490
<v Speaker 1>Honest withdrawal, how does that work?

407
00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:36.250
<v Speaker 2>Well, if you sit in the presence of the guide and smile, but inside your chest you are questioning his judgment and resisting his instruction, you are breeding a spiritual disease.

408
00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:37.210
<v Speaker 1>So you have to leave.

409
00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:45.130
<v Speaker 2>The disciple must physically withdraw from the company of the guide until the inward state comes back into harmony with the outward show of respect.

410
00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.970
<v Speaker 2>To sit there and harbor hidden doubt is not patience.

411
00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:50.330
<v Speaker 2>It is poison.

412
00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:54.450
<v Speaker 2>You withdraw, you correct your heart, and only then do you return.

413
00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:59.290
<v Speaker 1>The master then adds the 6th and 7th conditions to complete the catalog for the seeker.

414
00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:03.610
<v Speaker 1>The 6th condition requires guarding against the deceptions of the lower self,

415
00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:08.170
<v Speaker 1>by abandoning the gatherings of the ill-charactered and the ignorant.

416
00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:12.010
<v Speaker 2>The remedy here is concrete and social, not merely abstract.

417
00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:13.450
<v Speaker 2>It requires changing the room.

418
00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.010
<v Speaker 1>You literally have to leave the gathering.

419
00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:27.050
<v Speaker 2>The disciple must physically alter their environment to end the dominion of satanic influences over the heart, whether those influences come from the bad company of humankind or the invisible creation of fire.

420
00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:31.370
<v Speaker 2>You cannot maintain a pure state while sitting in a corrupted gathering.

421
00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:38.570
<v Speaker 1>And the 7th condition requires that in every circumstance the seeker must prefer humility and freedom from greed over wealth.

422
00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:41.770
<v Speaker 2>Embracing total contentment with the will of the law.

423
00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:44.890
<v Speaker 1>At this point in the letter, the disciple still has further questions.

424
00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:46.410
<v Speaker 1>He wants definitions.

425
00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:49.290
<v Speaker 2>He wants the theoretical breakdown of the inner life.

426
00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:52.490
<v Speaker 1>But the master answers them with startling brevity.

427
00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:56.810
<v Speaker 1>He demonstrates that the inner life is not a long, complicated curriculum.

428
00:24:57.130 --> 00:24:59.210
<v Speaker 1>It consists of four lived realities.

429
00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:00.890
<v Speaker 1>You either live them or you do not.

430
00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:03.610
<v Speaker 2>The disciple asks for the definition of the inner science.

431
00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:06.770
<v Speaker 2>The phonetic pronunciation for this science is Ta-Sa-Of.

432
00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:11.530
<v Speaker 1>And the master defines what it means to be a S-O-Ofi, using only two qualities.

433
00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:13.130
<v Speaker 2>There is no mystique here.

434
00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:17.770
<v Speaker 2>No secret handshakes, no private signs, just two uncompromising realities.

435
00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:18.370
<v Speaker 1>What is the first?

436
00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:24.250
<v Speaker 2>The first is obedience to Allah, which forms the outward perimeter by acting precisely in accordance with the law.

437
00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:24.970
<v Speaker 1>And the second.

438
00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:28.730
<v Speaker 2>The second quality is compassion toward all of Allah's creation.

439
00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:35.370
<v Speaker 2>Compassion here means sacrificing personal motives and performing selfless service free from self-interest.

440
00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:43.290
<v Speaker 1>So if those two qualities are present obedience to the Creator and selfless compassion toward the creation, the reality is achieved.

441
00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:43.850
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

442
00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:45.530
<v Speaker 1>Then he asks about servitude.

443
00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:47.530
<v Speaker 1>What does it actually mean to be a servant?

444
00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:50.650
<v Speaker 1>The master breaks it into 3 exact requirements.

445
00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.290
<v Speaker 1>First, obey the commandments of the law.

446
00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:57.250
<v Speaker 1>Second, accept the decree and destiny appointed by Allah.

447
00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:02.450
<v Speaker 2>And third, dissolve one's own desires and claims entirely into the will of Allah.

448
00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:04.930
<v Speaker 1>Think about the weight of that third requirement.

449
00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:10.970
<v Speaker 2>To dissolve one's own desires into the will of Allah means that the I want must surrender completely to the He wills.

450
00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:15.450
<v Speaker 1>If you are constantly fighting against what is happening to you, are not in a state of servitude.

451
00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:17.170
<v Speaker 2>You are in a state of rebellion.

452
00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:19.530
<v Speaker 1>Next, the disciple asks about reliance on God.

453
00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.330
<v Speaker 1>The phonetic pronunciation for this is Tawakkul.

454
00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:23.730
<v Speaker 2>The Master defines this

455
00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:26.090
<v Speaker 2>as a settled conviction in the decree.

456
00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:33.210
<v Speaker 2>It is the absolute belief that whatever Allah has decreed for you will reach you, even if the entire world conspires to prevent it.

457
00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:38.890
<v Speaker 1>And what is not written in your destiny cannot be obtained, even with the combined effort of the entire world.

458
00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:42.330
<v Speaker 2>Effort lives entirely inside this perimeter of conviction.

459
00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:49.770
<v Speaker 2>You must act, you must drive, but your heart must rest in the absolute knowledge that the outcome was settled before you began.

460
00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.690
<v Speaker 1>Finally, he asks about sincerity.

461
00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.850
<v Speaker 1>and the cure for showing off, or ostentation.

462
00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:59.770
<v Speaker 2>The phonetic pronunciation for sincerity is EQLS.

463
00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:02.730
<v Speaker 1>And the disease of showing off is RYAA.

464
00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.970
<v Speaker 1>The cure for this disease is delivered exactly like a physician's prescription.

465
00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:11.610
<v Speaker 2>The master explains that showing off is born from seeking the praise and admiration of people.

466
00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:13.770
<v Speaker 2>You want the town mayor to see your charity.

467
00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.410
<v Speaker 2>You want your neighbors to notice your long prayers.

468
00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:17.530
<v Speaker 1>Seeking validation.

469
00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:18.010
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

470
00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:21.450
<v Speaker 2>The cure is to correct your estimate of where power actually lives.

471
00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:28.170
<v Speaker 2>You must regard the entire universe as subject to the control of Allah and see all created beings as equivalent to pebbles and stones.

472
00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:29.930
<v Speaker 1>Let us examine that carefully.

473
00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.730
<v Speaker 1>You are instructed to view the powerful

474
00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:39.930
<v Speaker 1>people in your life, the wealthy, the influential, the people whose opinions you desperately crave as literal rocks.

475
00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:40.970
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

476
00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:45.450
<v Speaker 2>Pebbles and stones possess no independent power to bring you grief or comfort.

477
00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:48.250
<v Speaker 1>If a pebble cannot harm you, why fear it?

478
00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:51.210
<v Speaker 2>If a stone cannot enrich you, why seek to impress it?

479
00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:57.610
<v Speaker 2>Once you truly regard creation in this light, the disease of showing off vanishes instantly.

480
00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:01.370
<v Speaker 2>You no longer seek validation from the gravel beneath your feet.

481
00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:03.930
<v Speaker 1>We have reached the closing pivot of the letter.

482
00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:07.850
<v Speaker 1>The master leaves some of the disciples' remaining questions unanswered on the page.

483
00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.050
<v Speaker 2>He tells him that some answers are written in other books.

484
00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:13.050
<v Speaker 1>And some are of a nature that writing them down is not permissible.

485
00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:15.690
<v Speaker 1>But if you look closely, this is not a refusal.

486
00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:17.290
<v Speaker 1>It is a reassignment.

487
00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:20.570
<v Speaker 2>He reassigns the disciple from the page to the practice.

488
00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:24.810
<v Speaker 2>He demands patience by stacking 3 specific verses from the Quran.

489
00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:26.010
<v Speaker 1>What is the first?

490
00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:33.690
<v Speaker 2>First, Quran 49.5, commanding the people not to clamor outside the house, but to wait patiently until the prophet comes out to them.

491
00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:34.530
<v Speaker 1>The second verse.

492
00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:42.650
<v Speaker 2>Second, Quran 18.70, the strict instruction of the wise traveler who taught Moses, peace be upon them both, to Moses.

493
00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:43.770
<v Speaker 1>And what was that instruction?

494
00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:49.090
<v Speaker 2>Do not question me about anything until I myself bring it up and explain it to you.

495
00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:50.010
<v Speaker 1>And the third.

496
00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:54.490
<v Speaker 2>And third, the command of Allah not to hasten the signs before their appointed time.

497
00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.610
<v Speaker 1>So do not ask before the right time arrives.

498
00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:02.010
<v Speaker 1>The Master focuses on a single imperative verb from the Quran.

499
00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:05.130
<v Speaker 2>Have they not traveled the earth so that they might see?

500
00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:06.890
<v Speaker 1>You are commanded to travel.

501
00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:08.970
<v Speaker 1>Do not read books about traveling.

502
00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:10.730
<v Speaker 1>Do not ask after other travelers.

503
00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:14.570
<v Speaker 1>Do not stand still and dispute the geography of the path with the people around you.

504
00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.570
<v Speaker 2>Strive with your utmost at every step.

505
00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:17.290
<v Speaker 2>Just travel.

506
00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:21.770
<v Speaker 1>The master then seals this entire threshold by quoting the early Egyptian master.

507
00:29:22.090 --> 00:29:24.010
<v Speaker 1>May Allah shower his mercy upon him.

508
00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.730
<v Speaker 2>It is a soft hammer to the heart of the disciple.

509
00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:28.890
<v Speaker 2>It lays out the final ultimatum.

510
00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:31.850
<v Speaker 1>If you have the courage to exert your utmost with your soul

511
00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:34.090
<v Speaker 1>way, then come and step onto this path.

512
00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:37.610
<v Speaker 1>Otherwise, do not merely occupy yourself with the beautiful sayings.

513
00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:42.410
<v Speaker 2>Beautiful sayings without a soul actually offered in the fire of practice are nothing.

514
00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:49.010
<v Speaker 2>You must step onto the path with your life or stop pretending that reciting the sayings themselves is the path.

515
00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:57.050
<v Speaker 1>Take what is written here and steadily put it into practice and through that practice, those matters which you do not yet understand will become clear to you.

516
00:29:57.370 --> 00:30:04.170
<v Speaker 2>Take what is written here and steadily put it into practice and through that practice, those matters which you do not yet understand will become clear to you.

517
00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:11.130
<v Speaker 1>If you find yourself merely collecting the beautiful sayings of the wise, what is the very next action you must take to stop pretending?

518
00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:13.530
<v Speaker 1>and finally step onto the actual path.
